     REM, "del", '"enforcement-open"'),
    # The textual-pin case that lived here was retired 2026-08-05 with the assertion
    # it proved. It compared wf-remainder's regex SOURCE to a literal copy of
    # bin/check's — 2 of 8 readers, spelling not behaviour — and a faithful copy of a
    # wrong pattern satisfied it. Replaced by the corpus case at the end of CASES.
    ("remainder: every refusal reason is printed, not just the first",
     REM, "del", "refused = False"),
    ("remainder: the docstring states that A == B cannot see a marker",
     REM, "del", "CANNOT SEE A MARKER"),
    # THE ONLY SELF-TARGETING CASE, and it needs the split literal below to exist.
    # Every other case stores its payload here and deletes it from ANOTHER file, so
    # the payload occurs once in its target. This one's target IS this file, so a
    # plainly-written payload would appear twice — in the code AND in this table —
    # and `prove: every del payload occurs exactly once` fails it. Adjacent string
    # literals are concatenated at PARSE time: the AST sees the whole payload, the
    # file text never carries it contiguously, and the count in the target is 1.
    # Do not join these into one literal; that is the vacuity the sibling check bans.
    ("prove: each worker breaks a private copy of the tree",
     PRV, "del", "shutil.copy" "tree(ROOT, work"),
    ("prove: cases are applied to the copy, not to ROOT",
     PRV, "del", "target = work" " / relpath"),
    ("prove: a SIGTERM teardown unwinds the worker temp directories",
     PRV, "del", "signal.SIG" "TERM, signal.SIGHUP"),
    # ── PASS-MARKER-GRAMMAR (2026-08-05) ─────────────────────────────────────
    # Deleting the opening half of SACRED_RX leaves a pattern that still COMPILES
    # and still matches every legal block — it just also matches blocks that are
    # not sacred. That is the shape the corpus test exists for: the previous
    # assertion compared regex source text between two files and a faithful copy
    # of a wrong pattern satisfied it, which is how three readers drifted from
    # five unnoticed. This case must fire on the ILLEGAL half, not the legal one.
    ("grammar: every sacred-marker reader accepts the same blocks",
     REM, "del", '    r"^<!--\\s*origin:\\s*user\\b[^>]*immutable:\\s*true[^>]*-->$"\n'),
    # ── PASS-DEAD-SCRIPT (2026-08-05) ────────────────────────────────────────
    # Break the path-shape filter, not the feature. Removing `--dead-scripts`
