    # filter leaves a detector that still runs and still reports — on 184 tokens
    # instead of 79, every added one a backticked filename in prose. That is the
    # direction this pass actually fails in: not absent, but too loud to read.
    ("remainder: dead-script detection keeps both of its tuned filters",
     REM, "del", '            if "/" not in tok or tok in seen:\n'),
    # Break the CITATION, not the row. A deleted row would shrink the table and could
    # be caught by a count; a row that still reads like a pass while its evidence
    # points nowhere is the shape the admission rule exists to refuse, and it is the
    # one a reader skims straight past.
    ("passes: every catalog row cites a defect that resolves and has a detector",
     PAS, "del", "plan/dead-script-2026-08-05.md"),
    # ── the indentation ruling (2026-08-05) ──────────────────────────────────
    # Re-widen the COUNTER back to whitespace-tolerant. It still compiles, still
    # counts every real block, and silently re-admits the two template examples that
    # made a directive guard fire on a date format string. The regression direction
    # here is inflation, not absence — the count goes UP and reads like more coverage.
    ("grammar: counters and guards reject an indented marker; the mask does not",
     CEN, "del", "    r\"^<!--\\s*origin:\\s*user\\b[^>]*immutable:\\s*true[^>]*-->$\", re.M)"),
    # ── the ratchet (2026-08-05) ─────────────────────────────────────────────
    # Delete the INHERITED verdict. The ratchet still runs, still keys on findings,
    # and still catches real regressions — it just reclassifies every finding from a
    # newly added pass as one. That is the failure that makes a catalog stop growing:
    # adding a pass becomes the thing that turns the build red, so nobody adds one.
    ("ratchet: keyed on findings, with INHERITED distinguished from REGRESSION",
     RAT, "del", "    inherited = {k for k in new if k[0] not in known_then}\n"),
    # ── a detector ships with its fix (user directive, 2026-08-05) ───────────
    # Delete the reversal. The applier still detects, still displays, still removes
